/-!
# Relative degree ψ(N) of the field of divisor q-expansions

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.relfinrank_full_eq_dedekindPsi`
(`cab9a0f3-98f2-5f8b-b199-b15d58eaf279`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `538a4427-4bf3-52eb-94f1-b15c7aaed5eb`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a natural number assumed nonzero. Inside the field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of formal Laurent
series over $\mathbb{Q}$, let $jq$ denote the series $q^{-1}$ times the image under
$\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Q}$ of the integral power series `jNum`, i.e. the $q$-expansion of the modular
invariant $j$. Write $\mathbb{Q}(jq)$ for the intermediate field `IntermediateField.adjoin ℚ {jq}`,
and let `modularFunctionFieldFull N` be the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))/\mathbb{Q}$
generated by the set of all substitutions $q\mapsto q^{d}$ applied to $jq$, namely $\{\,$`qExpand ℚ
d jq` $: d\neq 0,\ d\mid N\,\}$, so the field generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the expansions
$j(q^{d})$ for the positive divisors $d$ of $N$. The assertion is that the relative degree, in the
sense of `IntermediateField.relfinrank`, of `modularFunctionFieldFull N` over $\mathbb{Q}(jq)$
equals `dedekindPsi N`, defined as the sum $\sum_{d\mid N,\ d\ \text{squarefree}} N/d$; since $d=1$
divides $N$, the smaller field is contained in the larger one, so this is the degree
$[\mathbb{Q}(j(q^{d}):d\mid N):\mathbb{Q}(j(q))]$, and the sum is the Dedekind $\psi$-function
$N\prod_{p\mid N}(1+p^{-1})$.

This is the degree statement equivalent to the irreducibility of the modular polynomial $\Phi_N$
over $\mathbb{Q}(j)$, here in the relative-degree form for subfields of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated
by $q$-expansions. It underlies the function-field description of the modular curves $X_0(N)$ and is
used in the subsequent degree computations and in the analysis of places and their prolongations
along $j$ and $j(q^N)$.

